00:07:24,545 --> 00:07:30,545
1500 slaves. 353,260,000 royal subjects. Warlords. Concubines. And
2 wives. He was the loneliest boy in the world.

2
00:14:49,090 --> 00:14:55,090
The Last Emperor is a drama, history film directed by Bernardo Bertolucci.

3
00:22:13,636 --> 00:22:19,636
A dramatic history of Pu Yi, the last of the Emperors of China, from his lofty birth and brief reign in the Forbidden City, the object of worship by half a billion people; through his
abdication, his decline and dissolute lifestyle; his exploitation by the invading Japanese, and finally to his obscure existence as just another peasant worker in the People's Republic.

4
00:29:38,181 --> 00:29:44,181
Pu Yi (Adult), played by John Lone.

5
00:37:02,727 --> 00:37:08,727
Wan Jung, played by Joan Chen.

6
00:44:27,272 --> 00:44:33,272
Reginald Johnston (R.J.), played by Peter O'Toole.

7
00:51:51,818 --> 00:51:57,818
The Governor, played by Ruocheng Ying.

8
00:59:16,363 --> 00:59:22,363
Chen Pao Shen, played by Victor Wong Chi-Keung.

9
01:06:40,909 --> 01:06:46,909
Big Li, played by Dennis Dun.

10
01:14:05,454 --> 01:14:11,454
The Last Emperor (Italian: L'ultimo imperatore) is a 1987 epic biographical
drama film about the life of Puyi, the last Emperor of China.

11
01:21:30,000 --> 01:21:36,000
It is directed by Bernardo Bertolucci from a screenplay he co-wrote with Mark Peploe, which was
adapted from Puyi's 1964 autobiography, and independently produced by Jeremy Thomas.

12
01:28:54,545 --> 01:29:00,545
The film depicts Puyi's life from his ascent to the throne as an infant to his
imprisonment and political rehabilitation by the Chinese Communist Party.

13
01:36:19,090 --> 01:36:25,090
It stars John Lone in the eponymous role, with Peter O'Toole, Joan Chen, Ruocheng Ying, Victor Wong, Dennis Dun,
Vivian Wu, Lisa Lu, and Ryuichi Sakamoto (who also composed the film score with David Byrne and Cong Su).

14
01:43:43,636 --> 01:43:49,636
It was the first Western feature film authorised by the People's
Republic of China to film in the Forbidden City in Beijing.

15
01:51:08,181 --> 01:51:14,181
The Last Emperor premiered at the 1987 Tokyo International Film Festival, and was
released in the United States by Columbia Pictures on November 18.

16
01:58:32,727 --> 01:58:38,727
It earned widespread positive reviews from critics
and was also a commercial success.

17
02:05:57,272 --> 02:06:03,272
At the 60th Academy Awards, it won all nine Oscars it was nominated for,
including Best Picture, Best Director, and Best Adapted Screenplay.

18
02:13:21,818 --> 02:13:27,818
It also won several other accolades, including three BAFTA Awards, four Golden Globe Awards,
nine David di Donatello Awards, and a Grammy Award for its musical score.

19
02:20:46,363 --> 02:20:52,363
The film was converted into 3D and shown in the Cannes
Classics section at the 2013 Cannes Film Festival.

20
02:28:10,909 --> 02:28:16,909
The film explores themes of suicide, experiment, china, isolation, buddhism.

21
02:35:35,454 --> 02:35:41,454
The tone is emotional.
